WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.620
the Arbuckle award
you know honors an alumna or

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:17.670
alumnus of the school every year who
who has made lifelong contributions to the

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:24.570
community and society and it's an award
about leadership and we're often reading

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:30.359
about leaders or organizations that are
falling short of what is expected and so

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:35.520
it's when we get to celebrate someone
who is an emblematic of what a great

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.100
leader can accomplish that's a that's a
wonderful thing to do and I think if

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.300
great leadership in many ways is is
defined by just a very few things it's

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.340
defined by the ability to develop a
vision and a strategy to execute against

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.469
that vision the ability to listen and
communicate in a way that motivates and

00:00:55.469 --> 00:01:00.480
inspires people around you and then most
importantly great leadership is defined

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:08.490
by integrity and principles that set the
tone and culture of organizations and so

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:14.490
it's fitting in that regard that we're
here tonight to honor Lorien for her

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:20.700
service to the GSB to Stanford to the
greater community for her work in

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.780
philanthropy and the work of Emerson
collective as she gets to join the

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.290
illustrious group that we recognized
before dinner it has been a great

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.710
pleasure for me personally to get to
know Lori in both as an alum and through

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:38.400
her work as a trustee she's she just she
she pushes us to really aim high for

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.060
Stanford she asks laser-sharp questions
she brings an incredibly wide aperture

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.329
to the discussion she thinks not just
about the School of the University she

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:51.720
always is asking you know how does this
matter for the country how does this

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.280
matter for the world last year we had
her come to the school and she she

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.939
talked in view from the top
what stayed with me after she gave that

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:03.719
talk and I and I'm sure it stayed with
the hundreds of students who were in the

00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:08.069
audience that day was the way that
Laureen had brought together you know on

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:13.380
the one hand just formidable
intelligence and an incredibly

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.390
and dynamic spirit and then coupled
those things with just a deep passion

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:23.990
for wanting to improve lives whether it
was through health or education or

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:31.140
freedom freedom of people and ideas and
the combination of just an expensive

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.910
vision for what you might try to
accomplish and an incredibly sharp focus

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.900
on how do you get things done sort of
exactly what you would hope for in

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.510
someone who came out of the GSB and has
gone on to be a great leader so it's

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.230
just such a pleasure that we get to
honor you tonight Laureen but with this

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:55.080
award okay now I have the great pleasure
of introducing Reid jobs so we graduated

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.780
from Stanford with an honours degree in
history and international security and

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.620
then a master's degree in history so
please join me in giving Reid a warm

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:08.210
welcome it's a really deep privilege for
me to be here introducing my incredible

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:14.700
mother Lorraine if anyone knows her but
you know that she's she's really a bit

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:20.010
of a polymath it's it's hard to nail her
down to just one description she

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:25.920
simultaneously today is a businesswoman
a philanthropist a social justice

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.760
warrior a mother of three and a very
successful beekeeper and producer of

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:40.410
honey and seriously but I've known her
really my entire life my very first

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:44.900
moments
at Goldwyn she really improved on a lot

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.790
of her natural abilities as a
businesswoman

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:50.799
and let me give an example she was at
Goldman when the Berlin Wall fell and

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:56.510
she really wanted to go she wanted to
see history being made and the the East

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.690
being opened up
now unfortunately she had just come out

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:04.670
of college with the you know and
couldn't really finance the trip so to

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:08.299
figure this out she offered her
superiors in Goldman a piece of the wall

00:04:08.299 --> 00:04:13.579
as long as they invested in her
transportation and in her housing and

00:04:13.579 --> 00:04:17.539
there was a lot of interest so she
syndicated about two to three hundred

00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:23.270
percent of her expenses for the whole
trip and everyone you know still has

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:26.990
their piece of the wall and they still
talk about it quite a lot so I myself

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.800
started working in cancer here in
Stanford when I was 15 in the cancer lab

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.340
and I started because of what it did to
my dad and what did to our family and I

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:42.680
still I still think about that a lot but
I I actually work on it now also because

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:49.130
I can do with her and it's actually not
so scary anymore so I couldn't be more

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:52.880
honored to introduce a really remarkable
woman my mom

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.660
Laurene Powell Jobs

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:07.260
well what a beautiful moment for me as a
mom and I'm so very very moved and thank

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:12.720
you dean levin and the GSP community for
this incredible honor I'm feeling truly

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:19.500
humbled I attended the Arbuckle Awards a
long time ago as a student and back then

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.270
I never imagined that I'd be standing up
here one day it wasn't that I lacked

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.830
confidence in my future it just didn't
seem like the kind of place where women

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.580
showed up
so part of the reason that I wanted to

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:37.110
be here tonight is to show the young
women sitting where I sat all those

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:46.200
years ago that of course there's room up
here for you but before I dive in I want

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.350
to ground my remarks and a few
principles that are at the root of my

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.490
perspective principles that we can all
agree on first

00:05:53.490 --> 00:06:01.160
poverty is immoral second extreme
inequality is unsustainable and third

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:10.080
inaction is inexcusable of course there
will always be levels of economic

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.480
inequality in a society but today there
are disparities of wealth so great that

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:20.220
they are an obscenity I understand that
in some ways a gathering like this might

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:25.020
be an unexpected setting to raise issues
of poverty and inequality after all many

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:30.210
of us are among the fortunate ones our
economy works for us our education

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:35.550
system works for us our health care
system works for us but we don't have to

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:39.660
travel far from this Hall to find a
community where all of these systems the

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:44.000
ones we know how to navigate so
brilliantly suddenly become calcified

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:50.910
two miles down the road in East Palo
Alto per capita income is $22,000 about

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:56.130
a quarter of what it is in Palo Alto I'm
raising this because the story of East

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:01.410
Palo Alto and Palo Alto of the
coexistence of prosperity and poverty

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:07.440
and our own community is a parable of
the situation in our country since 1980

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.460
the income of the top 1% like
mine have tripled while the incomes of

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:19.159
the bottom 60% of workers have remained
flat with 40 million Americans living in

00:07:19.159 --> 00:07:24.830
poverty that includes one in five
children now let me repeat that one in

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:31.000
five children live in poverty in America
shall we defend our democracy of course

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:35.750
but we must also recognize that the
voices of too many people in our country

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:41.560
are missing or suppressed in its
deliberations shall we defend capitalism

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.169
yes
of course but we must also acknowledge

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:47.960
the many Americans who are denied its
benefits

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:53.870
we need capitalism with a conscience if
scientists and engineers suddenly

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:58.520
discovered some rare and precious
material with untold potential does

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:02.569
anyone doubt that the private sector and
government would move heaven and earth

00:08:02.569 --> 00:08:08.719
to harness it why can we not see that
say Tsar and Martha's intellect and

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:14.919
their character are just this very rare
and precious material

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.830
comes back to say czar and Martha
because they're not only gifted students

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:27.259
who need a little help they're also the
talent that businesses need to cultivate

00:08:27.259 --> 00:08:32.760
they are the consumers businesses need
to grow they are the community members

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:38.399
who support companies need to thrive
over the long term and if we invest in

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:43.500
them if we focus on preparing them and
empowering them to contribute fully not

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.480
only to our economy but to our democracy
we have a chance at narrowing the gap

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.640
between the way things are and the way
they might be the way they should be

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:58.399
not only in East Palo Alto but across
the country

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:04.350
so here's my parting hope this evening
for the student sitting where I sat all

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:08.730
those years ago especially the young
women I hope you'll come back here one

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:13.110
day and tell all of us how you did it
tell all of us how you not only

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:17.339
rediscovered those lost values that dr.
King spoke about and not only address

00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:22.320
the changing needs of society but also
used every arrow in your quiver to

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.760
create the world you want to see to make
of it a brotherhood and a sisterhood for

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:30.230
all of us thank you

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:42.370
doing thank you for sharing those
thoughts of us and just that Retta bowl

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:48.970
message of passion and commitment and
vision and inspiration for the students

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:56.460
in the room just amazing hey thank you
maybe another round of applause for me

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.630
let me just thank everyone for being
here tonight it's always such an

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.630
incredible pleasure to have this dinner
and to get together all the different

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:07.480
generations from the GSB
and to recognize someone as

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.440
extraordinary as Lorien and the past
winners who are here so thank you all

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:17.449
for being here

